🐛 Describe the bug
We introduced a template function flat_to_float to broadcast a flag value to a float vector. The code is as follows. Let's call it func2.
template <typename T>
void flag_to_float(T src, float* dst, int64_t n) {
#pragma unroll
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    uint32_t* dst_u32 = (uint32_t*)dst;
    dst_u32[i] = src ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0;
  }
}
Before that, we had introduced a template function with the same name but convert a flag tensor to a float vector. Let's call it func1
// This function is used to convert bool or uint8 to float mask for
// vectorization. The caller needs to make sure the src represents TRUE/FALSE
// correctly.
template <typename T>
void flag_to_float(const T* src, float* dst, int64_t n) {
#pragma unroll
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    uint32_t* dst_u32 = (uint32_t*)dst;
    dst_u32[i] = *(src + i) ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0;
  }
}
The two template functions could confuse the compiler with which function should be compiled and linked if the caller code as follows. Because the template parameter of func2 could be translated as T=bool* while the template parameter of func1 could be translated as T=bool.
bool tensor[128] = {false};
float dst[16];
flag_to_float(tensor, dst, 16);
We have encountered the issue when we verify HF accuracy - #94657, #94656, #94651. To address this issue, we could refine the code by constraining the template parameter of func2 to be bool or uint8_t. The code could be as follows.
template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_same<T, bool>::value || std::is_same<T, uint8_t>::value, bool> = true>
void flag_to_float(T src, float* dst, int64_t n) {
#pragma unroll
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    uint32_t* dst_u32 = (uint32_t*)dst;
    dst_u32[i] = src ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0;
  }
}
